# Databricks SQL Cost Model: Warehouse Sizing for the Wind Utility

Your wind utility has 15 analysts who need governed access to Gold
tables — fleet capacity factors, turbine health summaries, compliance
reports. They currently each maintain their own CSV extracts and
the CFO sees different numbers in every meeting.

DBSQL fixes the "different numbers" problem. But what does it cost,
and how does it compare to Snowflake?

## The bottom line

| Scenario | Monthly | Annual |
|----------|---------|--------|
| **Serverless (Medium, baseline)** | **{{serverless_monthly}}** | **{{serverless_annual}}** |
| **Serverless + Monday spike** | **{{total_with_spike_monthly}}** | **{{total_with_spike_annual}}** |
| **Pro warehouse** | **{{pro_monthly}}** | **{{pro_annual}}** |
| **Snowflake equivalent** | **{{sf_monthly}}** | **{{sf_annual}}** |
| **Serverless, 30 analysts** | **{{scaled_monthly}}** | **{{scaled_annual}}** |

Serverless costs more per DBU but less in practice — it suspends
in seconds, so you pay for fewer idle hours. Pro has a lower rate
but longer startup forces you to keep the warehouse warm longer.
Snowflake is in the same ballpark at list price, but the comparison
is more nuanced than just compute cost (see Section 6).

**The rest of this document shows how we arrived at these numbers.**
Every assumption is explained. Disagree with a number? Change it
and rerun.

---

## Section 1: Query Workload Profile

Before sizing anything, understand how the warehouse will actually
be used. 15 analysts does not mean 15 simultaneous queries — it
means bursts of activity clustered around meetings and deadlines.

analysts = 15

### Concurrency pattern

Monday morning is the spike. Everyone opens their ops dashboards
before the 8:30am fleet status meeting. After that, activity drops
to a steady hum of ad-hoc analysis through the day.

peak_concurrent = 10
avg_concurrent = 4

### Query mix

Not all queries are equal. A dashboard refresh hitting a
pre-aggregated Gold table is nothing like a complex join across
3 years of turbine telemetry:

- **70% simple dashboards** — current fleet status, capacity
  factors, alert counts. Sub-5 second queries against Gold tables
  with good clustering. These are the bread and butter.
- **20% ad-hoc analysis** — "show me all turbines where capacity
  factor dropped more than 10% month-over-month." 5–30 seconds,
  hitting Silver or Gold with filters.
- **10% complex joins** — SCADA readings joined to weather data
  joined to maintenance records. 30 seconds to 2 minutes. These
  are the ones that stress the warehouse.

### Business hours

The warehouse only needs to be active during working hours. Nobody
is running fleet dashboards at 2am (the on-call pipeline handles
alerts — that runs on a Jobs cluster, not the SQL warehouse):

hours_per_day = 10
workdays = 22
monthly_business_hours = hours_per_day * workdays

---

## Section 2: Serverless Warehouse

Serverless is the default recommendation for analyst workloads.
Databricks manages the VMs — the warehouse starts in about 2
seconds and suspends automatically when idle. You pay per DBU
while the warehouse is warm.

### Pricing

Serverless SQL: $0.70/DBU on AWS Premium tier (source:
[Mammoth Analytics pricing guide](https://mammoth.io/blog/databricks-pricing-2/)).
This is the most expensive workload type, but you trade a higher
rate for lower utilization — no paying for idle clusters.

serverless_rate = $0.70

### Warehouse size

A Medium warehouse consumes 8 DBUs/hour. Medium handles 8–12
concurrent queries comfortably — enough for our average load,
and auto-scaling can bump to Large during the Monday spike
(Section 5).

medium_dbus = 8

### The key insight: warm time, not query time

You do not pay for the seconds your query runs. You pay for the
hours the warehouse is WARM — spun up and ready to accept queries.
With serverless, the warehouse suspends within seconds of the last
query completing. But in practice, analysts cluster their work:
run a query, look at results, tweak, run again. The warehouse
stays warm through these sessions.

Realistic warm hours are less than the full 10-hour business day.
Analysts take meetings, write emails, eat lunch. Activity clusters
into 2–3 concentrated sessions per day with gaps where the
warehouse suspends:

warm_hours_per_day = 6

This is a judgment call. If your analysts are power users who
query constantly, bump it to 8. If they mostly check dashboards
twice a day, drop it to 3. The number matters — try both
extremes and see how the cost shifts.

### Monthly and annual

serverless_monthly = serverless_rate * medium_dbus * warm_hours_per_day * workdays
serverless_annual = serverless_monthly * 12

At serverless_monthly/month, this is the cost of replacing 15
analysts' individual CSV extracts with governed, consistent,
always-current Gold table access. Compare that to the cost of
the CFO making a bad decision because everyone's numbers
disagree.

---

## Section 3: Pro Warehouse Alternative

Pro warehouses use the same compute but at a lower DBU rate.
The trade-off is startup time: 5–10 minutes instead of 2 seconds.

### Pricing

Pro SQL: $0.55/DBU on AWS Premium tier (source: same Mammoth
guide). That is a 21% discount per DBU versus serverless.

pro_rate = $0.55

### The startup penalty

With a 5–10 minute cold start, you cannot afford to let the
warehouse suspend between query bursts. An analyst who waits
8 minutes for a dashboard to load will never use the platform
again. So you set the auto-suspend timeout higher — typically
10 minutes — which means the warehouse stays warm through gaps
that serverless would have suspended through.

Net effect: lower rate per hour, but more hours billed.

pro_warm_hours = 8

### Monthly and annual

pro_monthly = pro_rate * medium_dbus * pro_warm_hours * workdays
pro_annual = pro_monthly * 12

Pro comes in at pro_monthly/month — only slightly different from
serverless despite the lower rate. The extra warm hours eat most
of the per-DBU savings. This is why Databricks pushes serverless
for interactive workloads: the economics favor fast suspend/resume
over cheap-but-idle compute.

### When Pro makes sense

Pro wins when the warehouse runs continuously — e.g., a reporting
server that queries every 5 minutes around the clock. In that
scenario, the warehouse never suspends anyway, so you get the
lower rate without the startup penalty.

---

## Section 4: Snowflake Equivalent

Every DBSQL conversation eventually becomes "so how does this
compare to Snowflake?" Here is an honest, numbers-first answer.

### Pricing

Snowflake charges in credits, not DBUs. A Medium warehouse
(roughly comparable to Databricks Medium) consumes 4 credits/hour.

sf_credits_hour = 4

On-demand Standard Edition: $3.00/credit. Most production
deployments use pre-purchased capacity at roughly $2.25/credit,
but we use list price for an apples-to-apples comparison (source:
[Snowflake pricing page](https://www.snowflake.com/en/data-cloud/pricing/)).

sf_rate = $3.00

### Auto-suspend behavior

Snowflake's default auto-suspend is 10 minutes (configurable
down to 1 minute, but Snowflake recommends 5–10 for interactive
workloads to avoid frequent cold starts). Snowflake cold start
for a Medium warehouse is typically 10–30 seconds — faster than
Pro but slower than serverless. This puts warm hours somewhere
between the two Databricks options:

sf_warm_hours = 7

### Monthly and annual

sf_monthly = sf_rate * sf_credits_hour * sf_warm_hours * workdays
sf_annual = sf_monthly * 12

Snowflake at sf_monthly/month. Similar ballpark to Databricks
serverless, which is not a coincidence — both vendors price to
the same market.

### The comparison is not just price

Raw compute cost is a wash. The real differences are:

**Snowflake advantages:**
- Mature, polished SQL experience — the UI, autocomplete, and
  result caching are excellent
- Simpler concurrency scaling (multi-cluster warehouses just work)
- Deeper ecosystem of SQL-first BI tools
- Time-to-value for a pure SQL analytics team is lower

**Databricks advantages:**
- Unified platform — the same data that analysts query in DBSQL
  is the data that engineers transform in Spark and data scientists
  train models on. No copies, no sync, one governance layer.
- Unity Catalog governs everything (tables, models, files, code)
- Better price-performance for large-scale joins and complex
  analytics (Photon + Delta Lake optimizations)
- The ML/AI story is significantly stronger

For a wind utility that needs SQL analytics AND predictive
maintenance AND compliance governance, Databricks is the stronger
platform play. For a team that only needs SQL analytics, Snowflake
is genuinely easier to adopt.

---

## Section 5: The Monday Morning Spike

At 8am Monday, 15 analysts open dashboards simultaneously. The
Medium warehouse — comfortable with 8–10 concurrent queries —
gets 15 at once, several of them complex.

### Auto-scaling to Large

Serverless warehouses auto-scale. During the spike, Databricks
spins up a Large warehouse (16 DBUs/hour) to handle the burst.
After the meeting ends around 10am, it scales back to Medium.

The INCREMENTAL cost of the spike is the difference between
Large and Medium for those 2 hours:

spike_hours = 2
large_dbus = 16
spike_additional_dbus = large_dbus - medium_dbus

spike_monthly = serverless_rate * spike_additional_dbus * spike_hours * workdays

### Total with spike

total_with_spike_monthly = serverless_monthly + spike_monthly
total_with_spike_annual = total_with_spike_monthly * 12

The Monday spike adds spike_monthly/month — about a 15% bump.
This is a common pattern: predictable spikes that auto-scaling
handles cleanly. Without auto-scaling (on a fixed Pro warehouse),
you would either over-provision all day or accept slow dashboards
during the meeting. Neither is good.

---

## Section 6: What If You Double the Team?

The utility acquires another operator. Now 30 analysts need access.
Peak concurrency doubles. You need a Large warehouse as the
baseline instead of Medium.

scaled_analysts = 30
scaled_dbus = large_dbus
scaled_warm_hours = warm_hours_per_day

scaled_monthly = serverless_rate * scaled_dbus * scaled_warm_hours * workdays
scaled_annual = scaled_monthly * 12

Doubling the team doubles the cost to scaled_monthly/month. This
is linear scaling — no economies of scale on compute. The savings
come from NOT duplicating the data platform: one set of Gold
tables, one governance layer, one set of pipelines. The marginal
cost of the 16th analyst is a larger warehouse. The marginal cost
of building a second data platform would be everything in Module 1
all over again.

---

## Section 7: Optimization Levers

These numbers assume no optimization. In practice, several things
bring the cost down significantly:

### Result caching (free)

Repeated dashboard queries — the ones 15 analysts run every
Monday morning — hit Databricks' result cache. If the underlying
data has not changed, the cached result returns instantly with
zero DBU consumption. For a fleet status dashboard that refreshes
every 60 seconds but the Gold table only updates hourly, 59 out
of 60 refreshes are free.

Estimated savings: 20–40% of dashboard query cost during peak
hours.

### Liquid clustering (fewer DBUs per query)

A Gold table clustered on `(turbine_id, reading_hour)` means a
query filtering to one turbine scans a fraction of the files.
Fewer files scanned = less compute = fewer DBUs. This is the
single most impactful optimization for query cost on large tables.

Estimated savings: 30–60% reduction in scan-heavy query DBU
consumption, depending on filter selectivity.

### Query tagging and monitoring

Tag queries by team or dashboard using `SET spark.databricks.queryTag`.
Use the Query History tab to identify the 5 most expensive queries
and optimize them first. Often a single bad query (a full table
scan that should have a filter, a CROSS JOIN that should be an
INNER JOIN) accounts for 20% of total DBU spend.

### Warehouse scheduling

Auto-stop the warehouse outside business hours and on weekends.
Serverless does this naturally (suspends in seconds), but Pro
warehouses should have explicit schedules to avoid paying for
overnight idle time. This is table-stakes configuration that
teams forget to set up.

### Optimized cost estimate

If you apply conservative optimization (25% reduction from
caching and clustering combined):

optimization_factor = 0.75
optimized_serverless_monthly = total_with_spike_monthly * optimization_factor
optimized_serverless_annual = optimized_serverless_monthly * 12

With basic optimization: optimized_serverless_monthly/month. Not
a dramatic reduction, but compounded over a year it is real money.

---

## Summary: All Scenarios

| Scenario | Monthly | Annual | Notes |
|----------|---------|--------|-------|
| Serverless Medium (baseline) | {{serverless_monthly}} | {{serverless_annual}} | 6 warm hrs/day, 22 workdays |
| Serverless + Monday spike | {{total_with_spike_monthly}} | {{total_with_spike_annual}} | +2 hrs Large on Mondays |
| Serverless optimized | {{optimized_serverless_monthly}} | {{optimized_serverless_annual}} | 25% reduction from caching/clustering |
| Pro Medium | {{pro_monthly}} | {{pro_annual}} | 8 warm hrs (longer idle) |
| Snowflake Medium | {{sf_monthly}} | {{sf_annual}} | $3/credit list price |
| Serverless Large (30 analysts) | {{scaled_monthly}} | {{scaled_annual}} | Doubled team |

### Try it yourself

Change `warm_hours_per_day = 3` and rerun — that is a team that
checks dashboards twice a day. Change `sf_rate = $2.25` to model
Snowflake pre-purchased capacity. Change `analysts = 50` and see
what happens to the spike.

The point is not to memorize these numbers. The point is to have
a framework for answering "what will this cost?" when a customer
asks — and to know which assumptions matter most.
